; Hex reaches the existing numeric refinements with no validator change: a
; hex literal *is* a number, so `:repr`, `:numeric`, and `:unit` judge it
; exactly as they judge a decimal. This case is the proof, on the boundary
; where it matters.
;
; Accept: `0xFFFFFFFF` is u32.max exactly, and `0xF` is the four-bit mask a
; WebGPU sample-mask idiom writes. Reject: `0x1_0000_0000` is one past
; u32.max and must be `repr_out_of_range` rather than truncating.
;
; `:unit (unit-shape :reject true)` is what used to catch this typo — the
; `0` with unit `xFFFFFFFF` was rejected for carrying a unit, which is a
; diagnosis about the wrong thing and only worked in schemas that opted in.
; It stays here to pin that the two checks do not collide: a hex value has
; no unit to reject.
(plugin :name gpu :version "1.0.0"
  (value-kind :name u32-mask
    :underlying number
    :description "A 32-bit write/sample mask, written in hex."
    :repr (repr-shape :type u32)
    :unit (unit-shape :reject true))

  (form :name target
    (key :name write-mask  :type u32-mask :optional true)
    (key :name sample-mask :type u32-mask :optional true)))

(target :write-mask 0xFFFFFFFF :sample-mask 0xF)
(target :write-mask 0x1_0000_0000)
